{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life began in the early 1140s and he eventually became one of the most powerful figures in the Crusader states. He inherited his title after the death of his father Raymond II who was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. Raymond III faced many challenges while trying to secure his position against Muslim neighbors and internal rivalries within the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond III was born as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He spent his youth under the regency of his mother because he was too young to rule alone. The Assassins killed his father in 1152 which left Raymond as the heir to the county. He reached his majority around 1155 and took full control of his lands. His early reign involved managing relations with the Zirid dynasty in North Africa and dealing with Nur ad-Din who threatened the northern borders of Latin Syria.\n\n*Political Struggles and Regency*\nThe political landscape of the Holy Land was complex during his rule. He became regent for Baldwin IV who was a young king suffering from leprosy. This role placed him at the center of power struggles between different factions within the kingdom. Raymond supported the peace party that sought to avoid war with Saladin. He also opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and Raynald of Châtillon. His position as regent allowed him to govern the kingdom effectively for several years while the king remained incapacitated. He managed to maintain stability despite the constant threat of invasion from Egypt and Syria.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\nTensions rose when Guy of Lusignan became king and rejected Raymond as regent. Raymond withdrew to his own territories and eventually allied with Saladin against the new king. This decision led to his exclusion from the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army was destroyed. He died shortly after this disaster in 1187. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. The county continued under his son Raymond IV but faced increasing pressure from Muslim forces. Raymond III is remembered as a skilled diplomat and a cautious leader who tried to preserve Christian lands through negotiation rather than constant warfare. His life reflects the difficulties faced by the Crusader states during their final decades of existence.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005644666}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.09029554}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.010377001}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.912786}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.02752608}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.093557686}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.14188956}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.44980347}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.09901251}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00035781108}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0017346876}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.033066068}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.016638122}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -5.4836423e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -5.4836423e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.043856643}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -1.5021127}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.043469794}, {"token": " began", "logprob": -4.27529}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.25676462}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.8011514}, {"token": " early", "logprob": -1.0064718}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.05207035}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.227225e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.41319588}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.83824426